        explanation: "- A tool's name and description are prompt text. Vague descriptions produce wrong calls far more often than bad schemas do\n- Anthropic's tool guidance: make each tool self-contained, give it a clear purpose, and minimise overlap between tools, because a bloated tool set creates ambiguity about which one to use\n- Prefer one well-shaped tool over three near-duplicates. `orders_search(query, status?, limit?)` beats `list_orders`, `find_order_by_email` and `recent_orders`\n- Return the smallest useful result. Large outputs consume the context that the agent needs for reasoning, and they push earlier decisions out of view\n- Paginate and summarise at the tool boundary: return ids plus a one-line summary, and provide a second tool to fetch one item in full — the just-in-time pattern\n- Error messages are instructions. 'Unknown status \"shipped_out\"; valid values are pending, shipped, delivered' gets corrected next turn; 'ValidationError' gets retried blindly\n- Make writes idempotent and require explicit identifiers. An agent will retry, and a tool that creates a second refund on retry is a financial bug\n- Mark genuinely read-only tools as such (the MCP `readOnlyHint` annotation, surfaced by SDKs) so the host can run them in parallel and apply lighter permission rules\n- Test tools the way the model meets them: with only the name, description and schema in front of you, can you tell which to call and what to pass?",
