        explanation: "- The structural problem of agentic systems is that instructions and untrusted data arrive through the same channel, so anything the agent reads can attempt to redirect it: prompt injection is LLM01 in the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications\n- Excessive agency (LLM06) is the companion risk: an agent with broad permissions turns one successful injection into real damage. Least privilege is the primary mitigation\n- Layer the defences: a tool allow-list, a permission mode deciding what needs approval, a deterministic policy check before execution, a sandbox limiting what the process can reach, and a human gate on irreversible actions\n- Deterministic checks beat model judgement for hard limits. A policy engine that intercepts each tool call — AgentCore Policy uses Cedar, and Kubernetes-style admission policies work the same way — cannot be argued out of a rule\n- Scope credentials to the agent, not to a human: short-lived tokens, per-session identity, and a separate least-privilege role per tool. Never give an agent a developer's credentials\n- Sandboxes matter because code execution and browsing are now normal agent capabilities: isolated sessions, no access to other tenants' data, and restricted network egress\n- Validate outputs as well as inputs. Improper output handling (LLM05) is how model text becomes SQL injection, XSS or a shell command downstream — encode and validate at the boundary\n- Bound consumption (LLM10): rate-limit tools, cap spend and turns, and alert on a session that suddenly costs ten times the median\n- Log every tool call with arguments, results and the decision that authorised it. Without that trail you cannot investigate an incident or prove what the agent did",
