        explanation: "- Evaluate the outcome first: did the agent achieve the goal? Deterministic checks — tests pass, the record exists, the file compiles — are the cheapest and most trustworthy signal\n- Then evaluate the trajectory: which tools were called, in what order, how many turns, whether any forbidden call was attempted, and what it cost. Two runs with the same answer are not equally good if one took 30 turns\n- Use an LLM judge only where correctness is genuinely qualitative, and bind it to a written rubric with examples. Validate the judge against human labels before trusting it\n- Build a reference set from real inputs, including the failures that prompted the work. Twenty honest tasks beat two thousand synthetic ones\n- Instrument with OpenTelemetry: a span per turn, a child span per tool call, and attributes for model, tokens, cost and decision. AgentCore Observability and other platforms consume OTEL-compatible telemetry directly\n- Track a failure taxonomy rather than a pass rate: wrong tool, missing permission, loop without progress, premature stop, refusal, timeout, injection attempt. Each has a different fix\n- Watch distributions, not averages: p95 turns and p95 cost reveal the tail where users give up and bills appear\n- Run the evals in CI. Prompt edits, tool-description changes and model upgrades are all changes that can regress behaviour silently\n- Keep a human review queue for a sample of production sessions. Recorded trajectories make the review fast and the findings specific",
